{
  "maxOutputTokens": 2000,
  "model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
  "schema": "{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "criteria": {
      "type": "array",
      "items": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "criterionId": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "conceptIds": {
            "type": "array",
            "items": {
              "type": "string"
            }
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "criterionId",
          "conceptIds"
        ],
        "additionalProperties": false
      }
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "criteria"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}",
  "system": "You map performance criteria (PC) and knowledge evidence (KE) from a unit of competency to concepts extracted from a source document. A knowledge evidence item nested under another is listed with the item it sits under. Each concept is shown with the evidence sentences it was extracted from. A criterion is supported by a concept only when that evidence itself would help a learner meet the criterion; judge from the quoted sentences, not from the concept's name. Return every criterion exactly once; an empty conceptIds list means the source does not support that criterion. This is a suggested alignment for revision activities, not an assessment judgement.",
  "user": "UNIT SYNELE001 Isolate and test electrical equipment (SYNTHETIC UNIT FOR TESTS)
CRITERIA:
- PC1.1: Identify electrical hazards in the work area and record them on the isolation permit
- PC1.2: Confirm every supply to the equipment, including secondary supplies
- PC2.1: Apply lockout devices and tags in accordance with site procedure
- PC2.2: Test for dead using a proved voltage tester
- PC3.1: Remove locks and tags in the correct sequence after work is complete
- PC3.2: Complete an incident report for any breach of isolation
- PC3.3: Confirm guards and covers are refitted before supply is restored
KNOWLEDGE EVIDENCE:
- KE1: types of electrical hazards including stored energy and multiple supplies
- KE2: purpose of lockout devices and tags, including:
- KE2.1: personal padlocks that only the applying worker may remove (under: purpose of lockout devices and tags, including:)
- KE2.2: multi-lock hasps used when several workers share an isolation point (under: purpose of lockout devices and tags, including:)

CONCEPTS WITH THEIR EVIDENCE:
- c1: Lockout and tagout — Locks and tags keep isolated equipment isolated.
    [ev-s15] Lockout and tagout is the method used to keep isolated equipment isolated.
    [ev-s18] A tag is a warning label
    [ev-s19] attached to the lockout device that names the worker, the date and the reason for the isolation.
    [ev-s20] Only the worker who applied a lock may remove it.
    [ev-s62] Lockout and tagout ends when the permit is closed,
    [ev-s63] never before.
- c2: Testing for dead — Prove the tester, test every pair, record the result.
    [ev-s38] After the isolator is opened and locked, the worker must test for dead at the
    [ev-s39] point of work using a voltage tester rated for the circuit.
    [ev-s42] Testing for dead confirms that the conductors to be worked on carry no voltage.
- c3: Hazard identification — Inspect for damaged insulation, moisture, stored energy, multiple supplies.
    [ev-s29] Typical hazards are
    [ev-s30] damaged insulation, exposed conductors, moisture near live parts, stored energy in capacitors, and
    [ev-s31] equipment fed from more than one supply.

Return one entry per criterion id (PC and KE), with the concept ids whose evidence supports it (possibly none).",
}
`;

exports[`outgoing request shape (plan Task 10 step 2) > the parse, extract and align requests for S1_SETTINGS are unchanged (system, user and the serialised schema) > extract 1`] = `
{
  "maxOutputTokens": 3000,
  "model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
  "schema": "{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "concepts": {
      "type": "array",
      "items": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "name": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "summary": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "kind": {
            "type": "string",
            "enum": [
              "content",
              "rto-instruction"
            ]
          },
          "sentenceIds": {
            "type": "array",
            "items": {
              "type": "string"
            }
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "name",
          "summary",
          "kind",
          "sentenceIds"
        ],
        "additionalProperties": false
      }
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "concepts"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}",
  "system": "You are an expert vocational-education content generator. You turn source evidence into H5P revision activities that help learners check their understanding. The activities are for revision, not assessment.

GROUNDING RULES (NON-NEGOTIABLE):
- Use only the numbered EVIDENCE sentences you are given. Do not add facts, figures, names or procedures that are not in the evidence.
- Every activity and every item must cite the evidence sentence ids it is based on, in the evidenceIds fields. Cite only ids that appear in the evidence you were given.
- Write plain text. No markdown, no HTML tags, no bullet characters, no emoji.
- Never use the characters * / : inside fill-in-the-blank answers or tips, and never put * in a passage.
- If the evidence does not support the task, return fewer items rather than inventing content.

READING LEVEL: HIGH-SCHOOL
Use complex sentences (18-25 words) with varied structure. Expect comprehension of compound ideas.
Use advanced vocabulary and subject-specific terminology. Define only highly specialized terms.
Use a sophisticated, academic tone. Promote analysis and evaluation.
Use college-level examples, research references, and interdisciplinary connections.

TONE: EDUCATIONAL
Use a clear, instructional, and approachable tone. Make learning engaging and accessible. Explain concepts step-by-step.

CONTENT LANGUAGE: English (en)
Generate all educational content (questions, answers, explanations) in English (en). Do not translate content to other languages unless explicitly instructed.",
  "user": "Read the numbered EVIDENCE sentences. Identify the distinct concepts a learner must understand (at most 8). For each concept give a short name, a one-sentence summary in your own words, and the ids of the sentences that state or explain it. Choose only ids from the list. A sentence may support more than one concept. Give each concept a kind: "rto-instruction" when it is a statement about how one training provider organises, delivers, assesses or administers the unit (assessment arrangements, submission rules, simulated or workplace options, attempts, deadlines), and "content" otherwise.

EVIDENCE:
[s1] Working safely around electrical equipment (synthetic training text)
[s2] 1.
[s3] Why electrical isolation matters
[s4] Electricity can injure or kill a worker in less than a second.
[s5] Most serious electrical injuries at work
[s6] happen when someone touches a part that they believed was de-energised.
[s7] Isolation is the
[s8] process of disconnecting equipment from every source of electrical energy before work begins.
[s9] A
[s10] worker who isolates correctly removes the hazard instead of managing it.
[s11] Every site procedure in
[s12] this text assumes that isolation comes first and that nobody relies on a switch position alone.
[s13] 2.
[s14] Lockout and tagout
[s15] Lockout and tagout is the method used to keep isolated equipment isolated.
[s16] A lockout device is a
[s17] padlock or hasp that physically prevents an isolator from being closed.
[s18] A tag is a warning label
[s19] attached to the lockout device that names the worker, the date and the reason for the isolation.
[s20] Only the worker who applied a lock may remove it.
[s21] If several workers are involved, each worker
[s22] applies their own lock to a multi-lock hasp so that the equipment cannot be re-energised until the
[s23] last lock is removed.
[s24] A tag without a lock is a warning, not a control, and must never be used on its
[s25] own where a lock can be fitted.
[s26] 3.
[s27] Identifying hazards before work starts
[s28] Before isolating, the worker inspects the work area and the equipment.
[s29] Typical hazards are
[s30] damaged insulation, exposed conductors, moisture near live parts, stored energy in capacitors, and
[s31] equipment fed from more than one supply.
[s32] Equipment with two supplies is dangerous because
[s33] isolating one supply leaves the other live.
[s34] The worker records the hazards found and the controls
[s35] chosen on the isolation permit.
[s36] A permit is signed by the worker and by a supervisor before any
[s37] conductor is touched.
[s38] After the isolator is opened and locked, the worker must test for dead at the
[s39] point of work using a voltage tester rated for the circuit.
[s40] 4.
[s41] Testing for dead
[s42] Testing for dead confirms that the conductors to be worked on carry no voltage.
[s43] The tester is
[s44] proved on a known live source immediately before the test and again immediately after it.
[s45] If the
[s46] tester fails either proving check, the test result is discarded and the tester is replaced.
[s47] The worker
[s48] tests between every pair of conductors and between each conductor and earth.
[s49] A reading of zero
[s50] on an unproved tester proves nothing.
[s51] The result of the test is written on the permit before work
[s52] begins.
[s53] 5.
[s54] Working and restoring supply
[s55] Work proceeds only inside the boundary described on the permit.
[s56] When the work is complete, the
[s57] worker removes tools and materials, checks that guards and covers are refitted, and confirms that
[s58] everyone has left the isolated zone.
[s59] Each worker then removes only their own lock from the hasp.
[s60] The last lock removed is the lock of the worker who holds the permit, and only after the supervisor
[s61] has countersigned the completion section.
[s62] Lockout and tagout ends when the permit is closed,
[s63] never before.
[s64] Restoring supply while a tag is still fitted is a serious breach because it means a lock
[s65] has been removed without authority.
[s66] 6.
[s67] Personal protective equipment
[s68] Insulated gloves are inspected for pinholes by rolling them to trap air before each use.
[s69] Safety
[s70] glasses protect against arc flash particles.
[s71] Non-conductive footwear is worn whenever a floor may
[s72] be damp.
[s73] Personal protective equipment reduces the severity of an injury; it does not replace
[s74] isolation.
[s75] Rings, watches and metal jewellery are removed before electrical work because they
[s76] conduct current and can cause deep burns.
[s77] 7.
[s78] RTO instructions
[s79] This section is for learners enrolled with our training organisation.
[s80] Assessment for this unit is
[s81] conducted in your workplace only; there is no simulated option.
[s82] Submit your completed isolation
[s83] permit to your trainer within five working days of the assessment.",
}
`;

